<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Need Two Papayas, Please. – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Local Farm Stand</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Need Two Papayas, Please.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Talk about people or things using simple language. Interactive: Role-play simple social interactions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can talk about buying and selling things in familiar places (e.g., "Do you have any apples? I want to buy two." "Yes, I have some."). Can role-play simple social interactions using polite language (e.g., "Can I have two mangos, please?"). Can ask questions about prices during role-play (e.g., "How much is this apple?").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 45%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of fruits and vegetables (e.g., papaya, banana, mango, tomato, lettuce) introduced in previous lessons. The teacher holds up each flashcard and asks: "What is this?" and "Do you like (papaya)?". Students respond chorally or individually. Teacher then asks "Where can we buy these?" eliciting "at the farm stand" or "at the market." Teacher prompts: "Today we will talk about buying fruits and vegetables."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models polite requests and questions for buying items. Teacher holds up a flashcard of a papaya and says slowly and clearly: "Can I have two papayas, please?" then holds up a banana flashcard and asks: "How much is this banana?" Teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /p/ in "papaya" and /b/ in "banana," exaggerating the sounds. Teacher also claps out syllables for "banana" (ba-na-na) and "papaya" (pa-pa-ya), having students repeat.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key phrases.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'Can I have two papayas, please?', am I asking or telling?" (Expected answer: Asking.) "What do I want?" (Expected answer: Two papayas.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'How much is this?', do I want to know the color or the price?" (Expected answer: The price.) "Can you say 'How much is this tomato?'" (Students repeat).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Local Farm Stand")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple dialogue script projected on the board or on handouts, set at "The Local Farm Stand". The dialogue uses target vocabulary (papaya, mango, banana, price) and grammar (polite requests, WH-questions, "some/any").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand and produce simple spoken language to initiate and respond to basic transactions at a farm stand, focusing on asking for items and prices politely.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Example:</b><br>
                <b>Customer:</b> Hello! Do you have any mangos, please?<br>
                <b>Vendor:</b> Yes, I have some fresh mangos. How many do you need?<br>
                <b>Customer:</b> I need two mangos, please. How much are they?<br>
                <b>Vendor:</b> They are one dollar each.<br>
                <b>Customer:</b> Okay, I want two mangos. Do you have any papayas?<br>
                <b>Vendor:</b> No, I don't have any papayas today. But I have some delicious pineapples!<br>
                <b>Customer:</b> Oh, okay. Thank you!</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the dialogue aloud twice. Students listen.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks gist questions: "Who is talking?" (Customer and Vendor) "Where are they?" (At a farm stand).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks specific questions: "Does the vendor have any mangos?" (Yes, some.) "Does the vendor have any papayas?" (No, he doesn't.) "How many mangos does the customer want?" (Two.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the phrases "Do you have any...?" and "I need some..." and "How much is...?" explaining their function.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using polite requests ("Can I have...?", "I need... please"), WH-questions ("How much is...?", "How many...?"), and "some/any" with countable/non-countable nouns (mango, pineapple, guava, egg, cheese, milk) in short, guided exchanges.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Fill the Gaps (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with sentence frames from the dialogue, missing key words. Students work individually to fill in the blanks using "some", "any", "how much", "how many", "can", "please". E.g., "Do you have ___ (any) apples?" "I want ___ (some) milk, ___ (please)." "___ (How much) is this pineapple?" After filling, students practice reading the sentences aloud with a partner, focusing on accurate pronunciation of /p/ and /b/ and correct word stress.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Chain Drill - "Do you have any...?":</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher starts by asking the first student: "Do you have any mangos?" The student responds: "Yes, I have some mangos." (or "No, I don't have any mangos.") and then asks the next student: "Do you have any papayas?" The chain continues, using different farm stand items. The teacher monitors for correct use of "some/any" and pronunciation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Mini-Dialogue with Picture Prompts (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher displays various pictures of farm stand items with prices (e.g., 2 bananas - $1.00, 1 pineapple - $2.50, lettuce - $1.50). In pairs, students take turns being Customer A and Customer B. Customer A asks: "Do you have any (bananas)?" Customer B replies: "Yes, I have some. How much are two bananas?" Customer A replies: "Two bananas are one dollar." They switch roles and items. Teacher circulates, providing feedback on politeness and accuracy.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-20 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Local Farm Stand")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Paired Role-Play: "At the Farm Stand". Students will work in pairs, with one student acting as the "Vendor" and the other as the "Customer". They will set up a small "farm stand" using flashcards or drawings of fruits, vegetables, eggs, and bread with assigned prices. Each pair receives a "shopping list" for the customer (e.g., "I need two papayas, one pineapple, and some cheese.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each pair receives a set of item flashcards/drawings with prices and a customer shopping list.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students decide who is the Vendor and who is the Customer. They switch roles after the first interaction.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The Customer greets the Vendor and uses polite requests and questions to ask for items from their shopping list and inquire about prices (e.g., "Hello, do you have any papayas?", "I need two papayas, please.", "How much is this pineapple?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The Vendor responds using "Yes, I have some..." or "No, I don't have any..." and states prices. They engage in a short transactional conversation, focusing on the target language.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher monitors interactions, encouraging fluency, natural communication, and providing gentle correction only if communication breaks down.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in asking and answering questions about products and prices, collaborative interaction, and using polite transactional language. Emphasis on completing the communicative goal: buying and selling the listed items.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher observes pairs during the role-play, making notes on a simple checklist for each student or pair, focusing on the learning outcomes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Farm Stand Role-Play" with their partner.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Students' speech is generally understandable (e.g., /p/ in papaya, /b/ in banana).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Students use at least 5-7 new vocabulary words from the list (e.g., mango, papaya, price, buy, sell).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of polite requests:</b> Students use "Can I have... please?" and "I need... please."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Asking about prices:</b> Students ask "How much is...?" appropriately.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of "some" and "any":</b> Students use "some" and "any" correctly in questions and answers about availability.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Students interact and respond appropriately to their partner.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket before leaving.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can ask for two items at a farm stand. (Yes/A little/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can ask "How much is this?" (Yes/A little/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One new word I used today is: _______________</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy for you when talking at the farm stand today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was difficult for you when talking? How can you practice more?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What speaking strategies worked well today to engage students? Were the communicative tasks appropriate for their CEFR A1.3 level? What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., more sentence frames, slower pace, more modeling) are needed for students who struggled with the speaking tasks? The integration of grammar points like "some/any" and polite requests was generally good, but some students need more practice with question formation. Students enjoyed the role-play, but fluency could improve with more varied partner work.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4, students will move from speaking about buying and selling to writing simple sentences and short paragraphs about their shopping experience at the farm stand, using the vocabulary and grammar practiced today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>